export const lessons = [
 {id:'supply-demand',title:'Supply & demand',category:'Markets',time:4,question:'Who decides what things cost?',intro:'Prices emerge from a conversation between what buyers want and what sellers can offer.',simple:'When more people want the same limited thing, its price tends to rise. When there is more to go around, the price tends to fall.',story:'Think of a neighbourhood coffee shop. A new office opens next door. More people want coffee, but the shop has the same staff and equipment. At the old price, the queue gets longer. A higher price can bring the number of orders closer to what the shop can make.',takeaway:'A price is a signal about scarcity—and an incentive for buyers and sellers to change what they do.',caveat:'This is a competitive market model, holding other things constant. Market power, price controls, slow adjustments and changing costs can all affect the result.',experiment:'A little coffee market',instructions:'Move demand or supply. Watch the point where buyers and sellers agree move with it.',quiz:'A second café opens nearby. If demand stays the same and total coffee supply rises, what does this simple model predict?',answers:['The market price tends to fall.','The market price must rise.','Nothing can change.'],correct:0,feedback:'More coffee is available at each price. With demand unchanged, the new meeting point has a lower price and a higher quantity.',source:'OpenStax · Demand and supply',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/3-introduction-to-demand-and-supply'},
 {id:'opportunity-cost',title:'Opportunity cost',category:'Everyday choices',time:3,question:'What does a “free” afternoon cost?',intro:'The real cost of a choice includes the best alternative you leave behind.',simple:'You cannot use the same hour twice. The value of the best thing you give up is your opportunity cost.',story:'You have six free hours on Saturday. You can work for €15 an hour or go for a walk with a friend. A three-hour walk has no ticket price, but it means giving up €45 of possible earnings. You may still prefer the walk—and that can be a perfectly good choice.',takeaway:'Ask “What is my next-best alternative?” before asking “Is this worth it?”',caveat:'Opportunity cost is the next-best alternative, not the sum of every option you reject. Time, enjoyment and wellbeing matter too; not every value has a useful euro price.',experiment:'One Saturday. Six hours.',instructions:'Choose how much time to keep for yourself. See the paid work you could have done instead.',quiz:'You choose a free concert over your next-best option: a €40 paid shift. Ignoring travel and other costs, what is the opportunity cost?',answers:['€0, because the ticket is free.','The €40 you could have earned.','Every activity you could imagine doing.'],correct:1,feedback:'The concert may be worth it! But the next-best alternative is the €40 shift, so those forgone earnings are the opportunity cost in this example.',source:'OpenStax · Choice and opportunity cost',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/2-1-how-individuals-make-choices-based-on-their-budget-constraint'},
 {id:'comparative-advantage',title:'Comparative advantage',category:'Trade',time:5,question:'Can trading help if you’re better at everything?',intro:'Being fastest is not the same as having the lowest opportunity cost.',simple:'Trade can help when each person focuses on the task that costs them less of the other thing.',story:'In one hour, Alex can make 6 sandwiches or 3 salads. Sam can make 2 sandwiches or 2 salads. Alex is faster at both. But one sandwich costs Alex half a salad, while it costs Sam a whole salad. Alex has the comparative advantage in sandwiches; Sam has it in salads.',takeaway:'Specialise according to what you give up—not simply what you make fastest.',caveat:'This model assumes fixed productivity, divisible work and no trading costs. Real trade involves transport, adjustment costs and who receives the gains. A bigger total does not guarantee that everyone benefits without an appropriate exchange.',experiment:'Two cooks. The same two hours.',instructions:'Compare a shared workload with specialisation. Then choose how many salads Sam trades to Alex.',quiz:'Alex is faster at both tasks. Why can Alex still gain from trading with Sam?',answers:['Being faster at everything makes trade pointless.','Sam must be paid less.','Their opportunity costs differ.'],correct:2,feedback:'Alex gives up fewer salads per sandwich, while Sam gives up fewer sandwiches per salad. Their different trade-offs create room for a mutually beneficial exchange.',source:'OpenStax · Comparative advantage',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/33-1-absolute-and-comparative-advantage'},
 {id:'diminishing-utility',title:'Diminishing marginal utility',category:'Everyday choices',time:3,question:'Why is the first slice so good?',intro:'More of a good thing can still be good—just a little less exciting each time.',simple:'Each extra helping often adds less happiness than the one before it. “Marginal” just means the next one.',story:'You arrive hungry at a pizza restaurant. The first slice is wonderful. The second is good too. By the fifth, another slice may add very little enjoyment. Your total enjoyment can still rise, even as the extra enjoyment from each slice gets smaller.',takeaway:'“How much do I like it?” and “How much do I want one more?” are different questions.',caveat:'Happiness points here are invented to make the idea visible. Utility is not directly measurable or comparable across people. Some goods, situations and early stages of consumption do not follow this pattern.',experiment:'The next slice',instructions:'Add slices. Compare total enjoyment with the enjoyment from just the latest slice.',quiz:'If the next slice adds less enjoyment than the last one but still adds some, what happens to total enjoyment?',answers:['It falls immediately.','It rises, but by a smaller amount.','It must stay exactly the same.'],correct:1,feedback:'A smaller positive addition still increases the total. Diminishing marginal utility does not mean diminishing total utility.',source:'OpenStax · Utility and consumer choice',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/6-1-consumption-choices'},
 {id:'paradox-thrift',title:'The paradox of thrift',category:'The wider economy',time:5,question:'Can everyone save more—and end up saving less?',intro:'What makes sense for one household can work differently when everyone does it at once.',simple:'Your spending is someone else’s income. If everyone cuts spending together, incomes can fall too.',story:'One household saves more by eating out less. But if the entire town does the same, restaurants lose sales, reduce hours and pay less in wages. Households try to save a larger share of income, but the income they share may shrink.',takeaway:'In the short run, an economy is a loop. A decision can come back around through someone else’s income.',caveat:'This is a stylised short-run demand model with spare capacity, fixed prices and fixed investment. It is not advice to avoid saving. Long-run investment, interest rates, government policy, imports and supply constraints can change the outcome.',experiment:'Follow the spending loop',instructions:'Raise the share of income households try to save. See what happens when investment stays fixed.',quiz:'Why might households fail to increase their total saving when everyone cuts spending at once?',answers:['Their incomes may fall as businesses lose sales.','Saving is always bad for the economy.','Banks automatically destroy the extra saving.'],correct:0,feedback:'If lower spending reduces income, saving a higher share of a smaller income may not increase total saving. The effect depends on the economic setting and policy response.',source:'OpenStax · Keynesian expenditure model',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/d-the-expenditure-output-model'},
 {id:'prisoners-dilemma',title:'The prisoner’s dilemma',category:'Game theory',time:4,question:'Why don’t sensible choices always add up?',intro:'The best choice for each person can leave both worse off than cooperating.',simple:'When each person protects their own interests, both can miss out on a better shared result.',story:'Two competing cafés decide whether to keep their normal prices or offer a discount. A discount wins customers if the other café holds its price. But when both discount, they squeeze each other’s profits. Each has an incentive to discount, even though both would earn more at their normal prices.',takeaway:'Outcomes depend on the incentives in the game—not just whether the players are sensible.',caveat:'This is a one-round game with illustrative payoffs. Repeated interaction, communication, trust and enforcement can change incentives. The café story illustrates game theory; it is not a recommendation that businesses coordinate prices.',experiment:'A small pricing game',instructions:'Choose for the two cafés. The first payoff belongs to Café A; the second belongs to Café B.',quiz:'In this one-round game, why is mutual discounting stable?',answers:['It gives the highest combined profit.','Each café does better by discounting, whatever the other chooses.','Neither café understands the game.'],correct:1,feedback:'Discounting is the better individual choice against either action. Both discounting is a Nash equilibrium, even though both keeping normal prices would give each a higher payoff.',source:'OpenStax · Oligopoly and game theory',url:'https://openstax.org/books/principles-economics-3e/pages/10-2-oligopoly'}
];
